ner, with z = −2 compared with the swimmer's z = −1.25","distractors":["The swimmer, because 55 is numerically farther from 60 than 68 is from 72","They are equally far below their means because both times are below average","The swimmer, with z = −4/5 compared with the runner's z = −2/72"],"e":"Standardization uses z = (x − μ)/σ. The runner has (68−72)/2 = −2; the swimmer has (55−60)/4 = −1.25. The runner is farther below the relevant mean."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.9","skill":"4.A","q":"Class A scores are roughly symmetric with median 78 and IQR 10. Class B scores are right-skewed with median 78 and IQR 18. Which comparison is directly supported?","correct":"Same median, but greater middle-half spread in Class B","distractors":["Class B must have the larger mean because it is right-skewed.","Class A must have the larger range because it is symmetric.","The two classes have identical centers and spreads."],"e":"The reported medians are equal, and Class B's larger IQR shows greater spread in its middle 50%. The mean and range cannot be determined from the given summaries."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.10","skill":"1.A","q":"A researcher plans to estimate the mean weekly exercise time of adults in a county. Which wording most clearly identifies the population parameter in the investigative question?","correct":"What is the mean weekly exercise time, μ, for all adults in the county?","distractors":["What is the mean weekly exercise time, x̄, among the sampled adults?","How many sampled adults exercise at least 150 minutes per week?","Does every adult in the county exercise the same number of minutes?"],"e":"The target parameter is the population mean μ for all county adults. The sample mean x̄ is a statistic computed after sampling and is not the unknown population quantity being estimated."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.10","skill":"2.A","q":"A study will compare blood-pressure change for two treatments. Which data must be collected to answer the comparative investigative question?","correct":"Record each unit's treatment and blood-pressure change.","distractors":["The treatment labels, without recording the response values","The final blood-pressure values, without recording treatment received","The population mean blood pressure with no unit-level treatment-response pairs"],"e":"A comparison requires knowing both which treatment each unit received and the response measured for that unit. Either piece alone cannot support a treatment comparison."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.11","skill":"2.A","q":"A university has 8 residence halls. Investigators randomly select 2 halls and survey every resident in those halls. What sampling method is used?","correct":"Cluster random sampling","distractors":["Stratified random sampling","Simple random sampling of individual students","Systematic random sampling"],"e":"The residence halls act as clusters: entire randomly selected groups are surveyed. Stratified sampling would instead sample some individuals from every stratum."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.11","skill":"2.B","q":"A district wants student opinions represented from elementary, middle, and high schools even though the groups differ greatly in size. Which design best guarantees representation from all three levels?","correct":"Take a random sample separately within each school level.","distractors":["Randomly choose several schools and survey the students present in those schools.","Post an online survey and analyze the first 500 responses.","Select every 50th student from one alphabetized district list without stratifying by level."],"e":"Stratifying by school level and randomly sampling within every level guarantees each level is represented. Cluster, voluntary-response, or unstratified systematic designs do not provide that guarantee."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.12","skill":"2.A","q":"A news site asks readers to click a link to vote on whether a new tax should pass. Why is the resulting percentage a poor estimate of support 